*
*
* START-DESCRIPTION:
*
*    result = !ps_script_out(script, want.out)
*
*    script   - PowerShell text.  Lines are field marks.
*    want.out - true to bring the script's OUTPUT back as well as its status.
*
*    Returns rc : @fm : the captured text.  Field 1 is exactly what
*    !ps_script has always returned; fields 2 onwards are the output, one
*    line per field, and are empty unless want.out was true.
*
* ***WHY IT RETURNS BOTH IN ONE VALUE.***  A function returns one thing, and
* the two callers want different halves: !ps_script wants the status and
* nothing else, the reporting verbs want both.  Threading the text through
* status() is not possible - status is numeric - and a second catalogued
* function would mean two copies of the PSTMP logic below, which is the part
* that must not rot.
*
* ***WHY THE OUTPUT NEEDED CATCHING AT ALL.***  PRE_RELEASE_FIXES 90: four
* shipped verbs promise "with no keyword it reports the current setting" and
* every one printed NOTHING.  They are administrator-gated, so they always take
* the elevated-helper branch below, and the helper returned a status and threw
* the child's stdout away.  Measured on the installed tree 31 Aug 2026 with
* append.sd.path and again with remote.api.
*
* ***THE SESSION PRE-CREATES THE OUTPUT FILE, AND THE ACL IS THE REASON.***
* secure-psdir.ps1 withholds DeleteChild on PSTMP so one SD user cannot delete
* another's file, and sdusers holds no rights at all on files it did not
* create - CREATOR OWNER is what gives each file to its writer.  So a file
* created by the ELEVATED HELPER would belong to the helper, and this session
* could neither read it nor delete it: an unreadable report AND a litter file
* nothing can remove.  Created here first, it belongs to us; Administrators
* keep (OI)(CI)(F), so the helper's child can still write into it.
*
* IT DOUBLES AS THE OPT-IN FLAG.  sd-elevate-helper.ps1 redirects only when it
* finds the file already there, so the pipe protocol is untouched - the reply
* to a request is still one line and still an exit code.
*
* ***AND IT IS OPT-IN ON PURPOSE, NOT FOR TIDINESS.***  !ps_script also carries
* !create_user, !set_passwd and !os_group.  Showing helper output
* unconditionally would put the internals of account creation on screen during
* an install, and PS_SCRIPT's own header records that scripts are written to a
* file precisely to keep a password off a command line.  Every existing caller
* keeps the old silence because !ps_script passes want.out FALSE.
*
* END-DESCRIPTION
*
* START-CODE

$internal
function ps_script_out(script, want.out)
$catalog !ps_script_out

$include int$keys.h

* 16 Aug 26 Windows port - the session's elevated helper, when it has one.
deffun elevate(action, arg) calling '!elevate'

*  ASSIGNED HERE FOR BCOMP, NOT FOR THE LOGIC.  bootstrap.py fails the WHOLE
*  bootstrap on an "is not assigned a value" warning, and BCOMP reads the source
*  in order.  Every value is also the safe one.
*  sys.f is NOT pre-assigned: it is a FILE variable, "openpath ... to sys.f"
*  is what assigns it, and seeding it with a string would be a type error
*  rather than a safe default.
   set.status 0
   rc     = -1
   out    = ''
   cmd    = ''
   out.id = ''

   if script = '' then
      set.status 1
      return rc : @fm : out
   end

*  PSTMP, NOT THE ACCOUNT DIRECTORY, and this FAILS CLOSED if it is absent
*  rather than falling back.  gpl.bp/PS_SCRIPT's header carries the full
*  reasoning: the account directory is sdusers:(M) and inherited, so every
*  other SD user could read the script - which for !set_passwd is a password
*  in clear - or rewrite it between the write and the helper reading it, which
*  is a local privilege escalation.
   id      = '$PS.TMP.' : @userno
   out.id  = id : '.out'
   ps.dir  = @sdsys : @ds : 'pstmp'
   ps.path = ps.dir : @ds : id

   openpath ps.dir to sys.f else
      set.status 1
      return rc : @fm : out
   end

   write script to sys.f, id on error
      set.status 1
      return rc : @fm : out
   end

*  THE PRE-CREATED FILE IS THE REQUEST.  See the header: it makes this session
*  the owner, which is the only way the output can be read back and the only
*  way the file can ever be deleted again.
   if want.out then
      write '' to sys.f, out.id on error
*        Not fatal - the script still runs, it just cannot be quoted back.
         want.out = @false
      end
   end

   q = char(39)

*  AN ELEVATED SESSION SENDS THE SCRIPT TO ITS HELPER INSTEAD OF RUNNING IT
*  HERE.  9 is "no helper for this session" and is the only answer that means
*  "not handled" rather than a result.
   if kernel(K$ADMINISTRATOR, -1) then
      rc = elevate('RUN', ps.path)
      if rc # 9 then
         if want.out then
            read out from sys.f, out.id else out = ''
            delete sys.f, out.id
            gosub tidy.out
         end
         delete sys.f, id
         close sys.f
         return rc : @fm : out
      end
   end

*  THE FALL-THROUGH: a session with no helper runs it here, and here the
*  output has always been reachable - "capturing" is what EDIT:684 uses for
*  its editor probe.  Taking it rather than letting it reach the terminal
*  keeps BOTH branches behaving the same way, which is the whole point.
*  Built into a variable first, which is the shape EDIT:684 uses -
*  "os.execute probe capturing probe.out".  An inline concatenation followed by
*  CAPTURING would probably parse, but this is not the file to find out in.
   cmd = 'Get-Content -LiteralPath ' : q : ps.path : q : ' -Raw | Invoke-Expression'
*  ALWAYS CAPTURE, EVEN WHEN want.out IS FALSE.  PRE_RELEASE_FIXES 115: a bare
*  "os.execute cmd" sends the child's stdout to SD's terminal, so an acting verb
*  (ssh.server install/remove, remote.*) that also prints its own sysmsg said it
*  twice.  Capturing routes the child's output into a variable instead of the
*  terminal - the same silence the elevated-helper branch has - and the text is
*  dropped below unless the caller asked for it.  This is what the paragraph
*  above always meant by "keeps BOTH branches behaving the same way".
   os.execute cmd capturing out
   rc = os.error()

   if want.out then
      gosub tidy.out
      delete sys.f, out.id
   end else
      out = ''
   end
   delete sys.f, id
   close sys.f

return rc : @fm : out

* ===========================================================================

*  ***ONE LINE PER FIELD, WHATEVER ROUTE IT CAME BY - AND THE TWO ROUTES DID
*  NOT AGREE.***  Found 31 Aug 2026 the first time a report reached a terminal:
*  it arrived as one long line studded with boxes.
*
*    - the HELPER route reads a directory-file record, and SD turns each
*      newline into a FIELD MARK on the way in.  CR survives, so every field
*      ended with one.
*    - the FALL-THROUGH route captures the child's stdout as raw text, CRLF
*      and all, with no field marks at all.  EDIT:686 shows the same thing from
*      the other side - it converts char(13):char(10) by hand.
*
*  Returning whichever the branch happened to produce would make every caller
*  handle both.  So both are normalised HERE, to the SD-idiomatic form: lines
*  separated by field marks, no CR anywhere.  A caller prints field by field.
tidy.out:
*  CR first, so a CRLF pair cannot leave a stray CR at the end of a field.
   out = convert(char(13), '', out)
   out = change(out, char(10), @fm)
*  A trailing newline becomes a trailing empty field, which prints as a blank
*  line the script never wrote.
   loop while out[-1, 1] = @fm
      out = out[1, len(out) - 1]
   repeat
   return

end

* END-CODE
